From: Nex package maintainers
Subject: read acpi event rules from three tiers, /etc winning

Source: Nex package maintainers
Upstream: not submitted; acpid intentionally has a single, admin-set
  ACPID_CONFDIR and treats layering as distribution policy

acpid_read_conf() (event.c) opens exactly one directory, hardcoded to
/etc/acpi/events, with -c as the only override. That collapses the
vendor-shipped defaults (power button, lid) and any runtime-generated
rules into the same directory an admin edits by hand, so a package
that wants to ship default event rules has nowhere to put them but
/etc, in violation of this distribution's package manifest policy of
keeping vendor defaults out of /etc.

Add a second entry point, acpid_read_conf_tiers(), that scans
/usr/lib/acpi/events (vendor), /run/acpi/events (runtime), and
/etc/acpi/events (admin) in that order, treating a directory's files
as authoritative over any same-named file from a directory scanned
after it. acpid.c calls this new entry point at startup and on
SIGHUP unless the admin passed -c, in which case it falls back to the
prior single-directory acpid_read_conf() so -c remains a full
override of the tiered list rather than one more tier. A symlink to
/dev/null in a higher-priority directory masks a same-named file from
a lower one, the same masking convention used elsewhere on this
system, without requiring a second stat of every file.

